\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Dials, hints, and the shape of the question}

Fix a large unknown prime $p$, or a semiprime $N = pq$ whose factors are unknown. A recurring question in computational number theory is whether classical arithmetic invariants leak usable information about the secret at cost polynomial in $\log N$. The paradigm is the quadratic residue symbol: for fixed $D \neq 0$, the map
\[
p \longmapsto \left(\frac{D}{p}\right)
\]
is, by quadratic reciprocity and its supplements, periodic in $p$ with period dividing $4|D|$. We call such a periodic statistic a \emph{residue dial}.

Periodicity is double-edged. It makes the symbol computable without knowing $p$: the Jacobi symbol $\left(\frac{D}{N}\right)$ is computable in $O(\log^2 N)$ bit operations by a Euclidean algorithm, with no factorisation. But a function of $p \bmod M$ takes at most $M$ values, hence distinguishes at most $M$ candidates and conveys at most $\log_2 M$ bits. If the observer already holds a hint $p \equiv r \pmod m$, the residual value collapses by a factor $\gcd(M,m)$. A dial is thus either simulable from the hint or informative, and the dividing line involves only $M$ and $m$.

The natural attempt to escape is to climb the reciprocity tower. Cubic reciprocity lives in the Eisenstein integers $\Z[\omega]$, $\omega = e^{2\pi i /3}$; quartic reciprocity in the Gaussian integers $\Z[i]$. Their symbols take values in $\mu_3$ and $\mu_4$, and their supplements are visibly not congruence conditions. Gauss's criterion for the cubic residuacity of $2$ at a prime $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ reads
\[
2 \text{ is a cube modulo } p \iff p = x^2 + 27 y^2 \text{ for some } x,y \in \Z,
\]
equivalently $4p = A^2 + 27B^2$ with $A,B$ both even. This is a splitting condition in the non-normal cubic field $\Q(\sqrt[3]{2})$, whose Galois closure $\Q(\sqrt[3]{2},\omega)$ has Galois group $S_3$. Class field theory identifies congruence-describable splitting sets with abelian extensions, so an $S_3$-condition cannot be periodic. The higher-power channel genuinely leaves the dial world. The question settled here is whether leaving it is worth anything to a computationally bounded observer.

\subsection{Results}

\textbf{(A)} The quadratic channel is exactly a dial: quadratic residuacity of $2$ is decided by $p \bmod 8$ (Theorems~\ref{thm:qperiod},~\ref{thm:qdial}), and every Legendre symbol is the reading of a periodic character of conductor $4|D|$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:legkron}).

\textbf{(B)} The cubic and quartic channels escape absolutely: for $M \mid 720720$, no statistic of $p \bmod M$ and no decision rule decides the cubic bit of $2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:absolute}); the same for the quartic bit (Theorem~\ref{thm:quartesc}).

\textbf{(C)} Escape does not raise capacity: a $K$-symbol fingerprint takes at most $2^K$ values at every exponent (Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}), the ceiling is attained at $K=2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}), the higher bit is sparser (Theorem~\ref{thm:sparsity}), and the composite-modulus predicate is symmetric (Corollary~\ref{cor:symmetry}).

\textbf{(D)} Hint plus dials plus higher-power bits still leave a large indistinguishable class, the higher-power channel contributing only $2^K$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid}).

\section{Definitions and basic apparatus}

Throughout $p$ denotes a prime and $\Z/N$ the ring of residues modulo $N$.

\begin{definition}[Power residue]
For $k, N, a \in \N$, say $a$ is a \emph{$k$-th power residue modulo $N$}, written $\PR_k(N,a)$, if there is $b \in \Z/N$ with $b^k \equiv a \pmod N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Power symbol]
For $p$ prime, $k \mid p-1$ and $\gcd(a,p)=1$, the \emph{$k$-th power symbol} of $a$ at $p$ is $a^{(p-1)/k} \bmod p$; its \emph{residuacity bit} is the truth value of $a^{(p-1)/k} \equiv 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Residue dial]
A \emph{dial} is a pair $(M,\chi)$ with $M \ge 1$ and $\chi : \N \to \Z$ satisfying $\chi(n+M) = \chi(n)$; $M$ is the \emph{conductor}. A family $D_1,\dots,D_L$ has \emph{conductor lcm} $M^{*} = \lcm(M_1,\dots,M_L)$ and \emph{dial vector} $\dv(p) = (\chi_1(p),\dots,\chi_L(p))$.
\end{definition}

If $M \mid M'$ and $a \equiv b \pmod{M'}$ then $\chi(a) = \chi(b)$; this is the only property of dials we use, and Theorem~\ref{thm:absolute} shows the escape does not depend on it.

\begin{definition}[Residuacity fingerprint]
Fix an exponent $k$ and bases $a_1,\dots,a_K$. The \emph{fingerprint} of a candidate modulus $N$ is
\[
\rv_k(N) = \bigl(\mathbf{1}[\PR_k(N,a_1)],\dots,\mathbf{1}[\PR_k(N,a_K)]\bigr) \in \{0,1\}^K .
\]
\end{definition}

The fingerprint is the \emph{secret-independent} read-out of the power symbols: unlike the symbol values, which live in $\Z/p$ and thus presuppose $p$, it lives in a fixed set of size $2^K$. This distinction is the technical heart of the negative conclusion (Section~\ref{sec:mirage}).

\subsection{The $k$-th power criterion}

\begin{theorem}[$k$-th power criterion in a finite cyclic group]\label{thm:criterion}
Let $G$ be a finite cyclic group of order $n$ and $k \mid n$. Then for every $x \in G$,
\[
\bigl(\exists\, y \in G : y^k = x\bigr) \iff x^{n/k} = 1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $x = y^k$ then $x^{n/k} = y^{n} = 1$ by Lagrange. ($\Leftarrow$) Choose a generator $g$ and write $x = g^j$. Then $g^{jn/k} = 1$, so $n = \operatorname{ord}(g)$ divides $j\,(n/k)$. Writing $n = k\,(n/k)$ and cancelling the positive factor $n/k$ gives $k \mid j$, say $j = km$, whence $x = (g^m)^k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Euler's criterion, all exponents]\label{cor:euler}
Let $p$ be prime, $k \mid p-1$, $\gcd(a,p)=1$. Then $\PR_k(p,a)$ holds iff $a^{(p-1)/k} \equiv 1 \pmod p$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} to the cyclic group $(\Z/p)^{\times}$ of order $p-1$, noting that a nonzero $k$-th root of a unit is a unit.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Residuacity tower]\label{prop:tower}
If $k \mid \ell$ then $\PR_\ell(N,a) \Rightarrow \PR_k(N,a)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\ell = kc$ and $b^{\ell}=a$ then $(b^c)^k = a$.
\end{proof}

Thus quartic residues are quadratic residues, while cubic and quadratic residuacity are incomparable; Theorem~\ref{thm:patterns} makes this quantitative. Residuacity is also closed under products: $\PR_k(N,a)$ and $\PR_k(N,b)$ imply $\PR_k(N,ab)$.

\section{The quadratic channel is a dial}

\begin{theorem}[Periodicity of the quadratic bit]\label{thm:qperiod}
Let $p,q$ be odd primes with $p \equiv q \pmod 8$. Then $2$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$ iff it is one modulo $q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Gauss's second supplement, $2 \in (\Fp^{\times})^2$ iff $p \equiv 1,7 \pmod 8$, a condition on $p \bmod 8$ alone.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[An explicit conductor-$8$ dial]\label{thm:qdial}
The function
\[
\chi(n) = \begin{cases} 1, & n \equiv 1,7 \pmod 8,\\ 0, & \text{otherwise},\end{cases}
\]
is a dial of conductor $8$, and for every odd prime $p$: $2$ is a quadratic residue modulo $p$ iff $\chi(p)=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Legendre symbols are dial readings]\label{thm:legkron}
For every $D \neq 0$ there is a dial of conductor $4|D|$ whose reading at any odd prime $p$ equals $\left(\frac{D}{p}\right)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Kronecker--Jacobi symbol $n \mapsto \left(\frac{D}{n}\right)$ has period dividing $4|D|$, and at an odd prime coincides with the Legendre symbol.
\end{proof}

\section{The escape: cubic and quartic residuacity are not congruence data}

\subsection{The witness modulus}

Set $\mathcal{M} = 720720 = \lcm(1,2,\dots,16) = 2^4 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 5 \cdot 7 \cdot 11 \cdot 13$. Every modulus $M \le 16$ divides $\mathcal{M}$, so one pair of primes differing by $\mathcal{M}$ defeats every candidate period in that range at once.

\subsection{Cubic witnesses}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:cwit}
$43$ and $720763 = 43 + 720720$ are prime, both $\equiv 1 \pmod 3$, and congruent modulo every $M \mid 720720$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:c43}
$2$ is a cube modulo $43$: indeed $20^3 = 8000 = 186 \cdot 43 + 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:c720763}
$2$ is not a cube modulo $720763$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Corollary~\ref{cor:euler} with $k=3$ it suffices to evaluate the symbol: $2^{(720763-1)/3} = 2^{240254} \equiv 632375 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{720763}$.
\end{proof}

Both agree with Gauss's criterion: $43 = 4^2 + 27\cdot 1^2$, while $720763$ has no representation $x^2 + 27y^2$ (a finite check over $y$ with $27y^2 \le 720763$).

\begin{theorem}[Cubic residuacity is not periodic at any modulus dividing $720720$]\label{thm:cnotper}
Let $M \mid 720720$. Then it is false that for all primes $p \equiv q \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ with $p \equiv q \pmod M$ one has $\PR_3(p,2) \leftrightarrow \PR_3(q,2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Instantiate at $(43, 720763)$: the hypotheses hold by Lemma~\ref{lem:cwit}, and the conclusion would transport Lemma~\ref{lem:c43} into a contradiction with Lemma~\ref{lem:c720763}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
No modulus $M \le 16$ decides cubic residuacity of $2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Absolute escape]\label{thm:absolute}
Let $M \mid 720720$, let $X$ be an arbitrary set, let $f : \N \to X$ satisfy $a \equiv b \pmod M \Rightarrow f(a)=f(b)$, and let $g : X \to \{\text{true},\text{false}\}$ be arbitrary. Then it is false that $\PR_3(p,2) \leftrightarrow g(f(p))$ for all primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$f(43) = f(720763)$ by periodicity. If the equivalence held, Lemma~\ref{lem:c43} would make $g(f(43))$ true, hence $g(f(720763))$ true, hence $\PR_3(720763,2)$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:c720763}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:absolute} is the conceptually decisive statement: the escape is not an artefact of restricting to integer-valued characters. No congruence-based computation of any kind produces the cubic bit.

\subsection{Quartic witnesses}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:qwit}
$137$ and $720857 = 137 + 720720$ are prime and both $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$. Moreover $96769^4 \equiv 2 \pmod{720857}$, so $2$ is a fourth power modulo $720857$; and $2^{(137-1)/4} = 2^{34} \equiv 136 \not\equiv 1 \pmod{137}$, so $2$ is not a fourth power modulo $137$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Quartic escape]\label{thm:quartesc}
For every $M \mid 720720$, every $M$-periodic statistic $f$ and every decision rule $g$, the composite $g \circ f$ fails to decide quartic residuacity of $2$ on primes $\equiv 1 \pmod 4$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
As Theorem~\ref{thm:absolute}, using the pair $(137,720857)$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:qwit}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Circularity, stated precisely}

\begin{theorem}[The cubic bit requires the exponent]\label{thm:circ}
There exist primes $p,q \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ with $p \equiv q \pmod{720720}$, $\PR_3(p,2)$ true and $\PR_3(q,2)$ false; namely $(43, 720763)$.
\end{theorem}

There are exactly two known routes to the cubic residuacity bit of $p$. The \emph{Euler route} evaluates $2^{(p-1)/3} \bmod p$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:euler}) in $O(\log^3 p)$ bit operations --- but both exponent and modulus are the secret. The \emph{Gauss route} decides whether $p = x^2+27y^2$ --- but for a hidden factor of $N$ this is a factoring-strength problem. Theorem~\ref{thm:circ} closes the third door, the congruence route, which is precisely the door through which the quadratic bit escapes to cheap computability. Escape from periodicity and computational inaccessibility are the same theorem read in two directions.

\subsection{Composite moduli: the symmetry barrier}

\begin{theorem}[CRT factorisation of residuacity]\label{thm:crt}
Let $m,n$ be coprime. Then $\PR_k(mn,a) \iff \PR_k(m,a) \wedge \PR_k(n,a)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) Reduce a witness modulo each factor via the natural ring maps. ($\Leftarrow$) Given witnesses $x$ mod $m$ and $y$ mod $n$, let $b \in \Z/mn$ correspond to $(x,y)$ under the Chinese Remainder isomorphism; since that isomorphism is a ring map carrying $a$ to $(a,a)$, we get $b^k = a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Symmetry]\label{cor:symmetry}
For coprime $p,q$ one has $\PR_k(pq,a) \leftrightarrow \PR_k(qp,a)$: the $N$-computable residuacity datum is a function of the unordered factor pair, hence cannot single out a factor.
\end{corollary}

\section{Capacity: the escape does not widen the channel}

\begin{theorem}[Fingerprint capacity, exponent-independent]\label{thm:capacity}
Let $k \ge 1$, let $a_1,\dots,a_K$ be bases, and let $S$ be a finite set of candidate moduli. Then $|\rv_k(S)| \le 2^K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The fingerprint takes values in $\{0,1\}^K$, of cardinality $2^K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Separation cost]\label{cor:cost}
If $\rv_k$ is injective on $S$ then $|S| \le 2^K$, hence $K \ge \log_2 |S|$. Equivalently, if $|S| > 2^K$ then two distinct elements of $S$ collide, for every exponent $k$.
\end{corollary}

The bound mentions neither $k$ nor the reciprocity law: the separation rate is a property of the number of bits read, not of the law they come from.

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness at $K=2$]\label{thm:sharp}
The two cubic residuacity bits at bases $2$ and $3$ are injective on $S = \{7,31,61,307\}$, a set of exactly $2^2$ primes; hence Corollary~\ref{cor:cost} is attained, and no set of five or more primes admits an injective two-bit cubic fingerprint.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Direct evaluation:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$p$ & $\PR_3(p,2)$ & $\PR_3(p,3)$ & fingerprint \\
\midrule
$7$ & false & false & $(0,0)$ \\
$31$ & true ($4^3 = 64 \equiv 2$) & false & $(1,0)$ \\
$61$ & false & true ($4^3 = 64 \equiv 3$) & $(0,1)$ \\
$307$ & true ($52^3 \equiv 2$) & true ($192^3 \equiv 3$) & $(1,1)$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
All four patterns occur, so the map is injective; the upper bound is Corollary~\ref{cor:cost}.
\end{proof}

Gauss's criterion agrees: $31 = 2^2+27$ and $307 = 8^2 + 27\cdot 3^2$, while $7$ and $61$ are not of the form $x^2+27y^2$.

\begin{theorem}[Sparsity of $k$-th powers]\label{thm:sparsity}
Let $p$ be prime and $k \mid p-1$. Then exactly $(p-1)/k$ of the $p-1$ units modulo $p$ are $k$-th powers.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $k$-th power map is an endomorphism of the cyclic group $(\Z/p)^{\times}$ of order $n=p-1$; its image has index $\gcd(k,n) = k$, hence cardinality $n/k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $6 \mid p-1$ then the cubes among the units number $(p-1)/3$ and the squares $(p-1)/2$, so the cubic bit is strictly sparser than the quadratic one.
\end{corollary}

Information-theoretically a bit of success probability $1/3$ carries $H(1/3) \approx 0.918$ bits against exactly $1$ bit at probability $1/2$: per symbol the cubic channel is strictly poorer, while sharing the same worst-case ceiling.

\begin{theorem}[All four joint patterns occur]\label{thm:patterns}
Among primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$, all four combinations of (quadratic bit of $2$, cubic bit of $2$) are realised: $7$ (square, non-cube), $13$ (neither), $31$ (both), $43$ (cube, non-square).
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Mutual independence]\label{cor:transverse}
There is no function $g$ with $\PR_3(p,2) \leftrightarrow g(\PR_2(p,2))$ for all primes $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ --- the primes $7$ and $31$ share a quadratic bit and differ in the cubic bit. Symmetrically there is no $g$ with $\PR_2(p,2) \leftrightarrow g(\PR_3(p,2))$: $31$ and $43$ share a cubic bit and differ in the quadratic bit.
\end{corollary}

The channels are transverse --- the cubic bit is not redundant --- which is why the joint bound below is a product rather than a maximum. But transversality is not amplification: $K$ transverse bits are still $K$ bits.

\section{The hybrid bound}

\begin{lemma}[Fibrewise pigeonhole]\label{lem:pigeon}
Let $S$ be a finite nonempty set and $f : S \to B$ any map. Then some value $v \in f(S)$ satisfies $|S| \le |\{s \in S : f(s)=v\}| \cdot |f(S)|$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $S$ into fibres over $f(S)$ and take $v$ maximising the fibre size; the sum of $|f(S)|$ terms, each at most the maximum, equals $|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Hybrid no-amplification]\label{thm:hybrid}
Let $\Omega$ be a nonempty finite candidate set contained in one hint class $p \equiv r \pmod m$, $m \ge 1$. Let $D_1,\dots,D_L$ be dials with conductor lcm $M^{*}$ and dial vector $\dv$, and let $\rv_k$ be a $K$-symbol fingerprint at any exponent and any bases. Then there is a joint reading $(v,w)$ with
\[
|\Omega| \;\le\; \bigl|\{p \in \Omega : \dv(p) = v \ \text{and}\ \rv_k(p) = w\}\bigr| \cdot \Bigl(\frac{M^{*}}{\gcd(M^{*},m)} \cdot 2^{K}\Bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:pigeon} to $F(p) = (\dv(p), \rv_k(p))$. The image of $F$ embeds in the product of the component images. The dial component is bounded by $M^{*}/\gcd(M^{*},m)$, since all candidates share a residue modulo $m$ so their residues modulo $M^{*}$ lie in at most that many classes, and $\dv$ factors through the residue modulo $M^{*}$. The fingerprint component is bounded by $2^K$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity}).
\end{proof}

Read as an indistinguishability statement: at least $|\Omega| / \bigl((M^{*}/\gcd(M^{*},m))\cdot 2^K\bigr)$ candidates share every piece of information the observer holds. The higher-power channel enters only through $2^K$ --- exactly the contribution $K$ bits from any source would make. Replacing cubic bits by quadratic bits at $K$ new bases changes nothing.

\section{The mirage of full-symbol separation}\label{sec:mirage}

For the $68$ primes $p \in [1000,2000]$ with $p \equiv 1 \pmod 3$ and the bases $2,3,5,7,11$, the vector of full cubic symbol values $\bigl(a_i^{(p-1)/3} \bmod p\bigr)_{i\le 5}$ separates all $68$ primes, and so does the quadratic analogue. Perfect separation with five symbols looks like a powerful attack; it is not.

Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} applies to the fingerprint, whose values live in the fixed set $\{0,1\}^K$. It does not apply to raw symbol values, which live in $\Z/p$ --- a set whose very description contains the secret. A statistic valued in $\Z/p$ separates distinct primes trivially because it \emph{encodes} $p$. The ``$68/68$'' is circularity in numerical costume. Restricting to the honest read-out:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
fingerprint (bases $2,3,5,7,11$; $68$ primes) & distinct values & capacity \\
\midrule
full quadratic symbol values & $68/68$ & unbounded --- values live in $\Z/p$ \\
full cubic symbol values & $68/68$ & unbounded --- same artefact \\
quadratic residuacity bits & $31/68$ & $2^5 = 32$ \\
cubic residuacity bits & $23/68$ & $2^5 = 32$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Both bit-channels sit under the ceiling $32$. The quadratic bits nearly saturate it; the cubic bits reach only $23$, consistent with the bias predicted by Theorem~\ref{thm:sparsity}.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{The closed accounting}

For a secret prime $p$,
\[
\text{residue-channel information} \;=\; \underbrace{\text{dial part}}_{\text{determined by } p \bmod M^{*}} \;+\; \underbrace{\text{fine-arithmetic part}}_{\text{non-periodic, needs } p}.
\]
The dial part is bounded by $\log_2 M^{*}$ bits and is exactly what a partial hint erodes (Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid}). The fine-arithmetic part is genuinely new (Corollary~\ref{cor:transverse}) and genuinely non-periodic (Theorem~\ref{thm:absolute}), but is obtainable only through the exponent $(p-1)/k$ or a factoring-strength representation problem, and is capped at $2^K$ patterns in any case. There is no third component.

\subsection{Why the escape had to close the door}

The escape from periodicity is a consequence of non-abelian structure: cubic residuacity of $2$ is the splitting condition of the $S_3$-extension $\Q(\sqrt[3]{2},\omega)$, and by class field theory only abelian splitting sets are unions of ray classes. Congruence computability and abelian structure are the same phenomenon; the cheapness of the Legendre symbol is the cheapness of an abelian character. Hence \emph{any} channel escaping periodicity is, for the same reason, not computable by congruence methods.

\subsection{Comparison}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
feature & quadratic & cubic / quartic \\
\midrule
periodic in $p$? & yes, conductor $8$ for base $2$ & no, at any $M \mid 720720$ \\
computable without $p$? & yes, $O(\log^2 N)$ & no known route avoiding $p$ or factoring \\
$N$-computable version & symmetric in the factors & symmetric in the factors \\
density of residues & $1/2$ & $1/k$ \\
capacity of $K$ bits & $2^K$ & $2^K$, attained at $K=2$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Every row that improves in the second column is paid for by a row that worsens.

\subsection{Limitations}

The escape theorems are proved for $M \mid 720720$; unbounded escape is Conjecture~\ref{conj:A}. Sharpness is exhibited at $K = 2$; general attainment is Conjecture~\ref{conj:B}. The analysis concerns residuacity bits as the read-out; a use of the raw symbol value that does not presuppose $p$ would fall outside it, but is exactly what the circularity argument forbids by the known routes.

\subsection{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Unbounded escape]\label{conj:A}
For every modulus $M \ge 1$ there are primes $p \equiv q \pmod M$, both $\equiv 1 \pmod 3$, with $2$ a cube modulo $p$ and not modulo $q$.
\end{conjecture}
Proved here for every $M \mid 720720$. The general case should follow from the fact that $2$ is a cube modulo $p$ exactly when $p = x^2+27y^2$, a splitting condition in the non-abelian field $\Q(\sqrt[3]{2},\omega)$: a congruence condition would force the splitting set of a non-normal cubic field to be a union of ray classes, contradicting the Galois group $S_3$. What is wanted is a Chebotarev-free argument; it suffices to exhibit, for each $M$, a pair of primes in one class with different splitting behaviour, and the $x^2+27y^2$ parametrisation makes such pairs constructible rather than merely dense.

\begin{conjecture}[Exact capacity, not just the ceiling]\label{conj:B}
For bases $a_1,\dots,a_K$ multiplicatively independent modulo cubes, the cubic residuacity fingerprint attains all $2^K$ patterns on primes below $\exp(O(K))$.
\end{conjecture}
Attainment is proved at $K=2$ with $\{7,31,61,307\}$. The joint distribution of cubic bits should be governed by the Galois group of $\Q(\omega,\sqrt[3]{a_1},\dots,\sqrt[3]{a_K})$, which is $(\Z/3)^K \rtimes \Z/2$ for independent bases; equidistribution then forces every pattern with density $3^{-K}$.

\begin{question}[Beyond bits]
Is there any read-out of the power symbol, other than the residuacity bit, living in a secret-independent value set?
\end{question}

\begin{question}[Higher $k$]
As $k$ grows the bit says ``yes'' on a $1/k$ fraction. Is there an optimal $k$ for a fixed query budget, and does it scale with the candidate set size?
\end{question}

\begin{question}[Non-abelian channels generally]
Does any Artin symbol at a non-abelian extension provide a polynomial-time handle, or is the escape/inaccessibility duality a theorem about all of them?
\end{question}

\section{Conclusion}

Higher-power reciprocity was a well-motivated candidate for a stronger residue channel: its symbols are finer than the Legendre symbol and its criteria are provably not congruence conditions. Both halves of that motivation are correct, and both are proved here. The cubic residuacity bit of $2$ is not computable from $p \bmod M$ for any $M \mid \lcm(1,\dots,16)$, by any statistic and any decision rule, witnessed by $43$ and $720763$; the quartic bit fails likewise at $137$ and $720857$; and neither of the cubic and quadratic bits is a function of the other. Yet the capacity is unchanged: a $K$-symbol fingerprint takes at most $2^K$ values at every exponent, this ceiling is attained at $K=2$, the higher bit is strictly sparser hence individually poorer, the composite-modulus version is symmetric in the factors, and the joint hint-plus-dials-plus-bits bound admits the higher-power channel only through $2^K$. The residue channel carries a dial part and fine-arithmetic noise, and nothing else.

